# Upstream kernel config seed pin — line @SERIES@.
#
# GENERATED HEADER: tools/advance-line.sh wrote this file from
# tools/seed-header.tmpl when the @SERIES@ line was stood up. tools/bump-seed.sh
# rewrites only the pin VALUES below and never this prose, so keep anything
# that goes stale on a bump (the pinned tag, dates) out of this comment.
#
# We seed our build's .config from anthraxx's (Levente Polyak's) own Arch
# Linux packaging repo for linux-hardened, signed-commit-verified against his
# key (E240B57E…) + sha256 of config.x86_64 at the pinned commit.
#
# The pin is a SAME-LINE @SERIES@ config. It routinely lags the kernel we build
# by a patch level — anthraxx changes config.x86_64 in only ~1 in 4–5 tags, so
# his newest published config is usually a few releases behind the tag being
# built. That is expected and handled, not gated: the seed is only a baseline.
# `make olddefconfig` reconciles it to the tree being built, the fidelity
# assertion enforces every configs/intent.*.config pin, and
# tools/check-hardening-drift.sh diffs the result against the most recent
# published @SERIES@ final.config in releases/ — so a symbol we INHERIT from the
# seed (rather than pin in intent) cannot silently regress across a seed bump.
#
# The FIRST @SERIES@ build has no same-line drift baseline yet: build-kernel.yml
# logs a "hardening-drift guard SKIPPED" warning for it rather than comparing
# against the previous line's final.config. Once that release publishes, its
# final.config becomes the baseline for every later @SERIES@ build.
#
# BRIDGED lines. When anthraxx has not packaged a @SERIES@ config.x86_64 yet,
# `tools/advance-line.sh @SERIES@ --bridge` stands the line up on the previous
# line's pin instead and adds a `# cross-series-seed:` marker block just above
# the values. While that block is there: validate.yml accepts the cross-series
# pin, the manifest records `cross_series_from`, and the first build drift-
# checks against the previous line's newest release (listing every hardening
# symbol @SERIES@ added) instead of skipping. seed-bump.yml moves the pin to his
# first @SERIES@ config when it ships, and tools/bump-seed.sh then drops the
# block. Never write the marker by hand on a same-series pin; validate.yml
# rejects that.
#
# Pinning model, bump procedure, line advance: docs/operators/config-architecture.md.

